      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Many languages use different words for *not exactly* (with numbers), *in a circle*, and *here and there*; English **around** covers all of them and also means *nearby* (*Is Sam around?*) and *existing* (*It has been around for years*).",
    "grammar": "Before a time, **around** can stand alone (*I will come around six*) or follow **at** (*at around six*); both are common. No other preposition is needed before the number.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two syllables with the stress on the second: *uh-ROUND*. The **ou** sounds like the **ou** in **out**."
  },
